<h1>Query TNS offline</h1>

<p>
  The <a href="{{ site.tns }}" rel="noopener">IAU Transient Name Server</a>
  publishes its full object catalogue as a daily CSV snapshot plus hourly delta
  files. <strong>tns-mirror</strong> downloads them, authenticates properly, and
  keeps a Postgres table in sync — so you can cone-search, cross-match and join
  against TNS locally, with no rate-limited API on your hot path.
</p>

<div class="callout" data-callout="warning">
  <p>
    <strong>This is not an official TNS service.</strong> It is an
    independently-operated mirror <em>of</em> the TNS public catalogue. TNS's
    data-use terms and citation requirements apply to the data.
  </p>
</div>

<h2 id="which-half-is-yours">Which half is yours?</h2>

<p>
  The project is two pieces with two audiences, and most people only need one of
  them.
</p>

<ul class="auto-grid list-bare" role="list" style="--auto-grid-min: 18rem">
  <li class="card flow" style="--flow-space: var(--space-2xs)">
    <p class="card__eyebrow">Server</p>
    <h3><a href="{{ '/quickstart/' | url }}">I want to run a mirror</a></h3>
    <p>
      You have a TNS account and somewhere to run Docker. Bring up the server
      with a compose file and a <code>.env</code>, and it keeps a Postgres
      database current on its own.
    </p>
  </li>
  <li class="card flow" style="--flow-space: var(--space-2xs)">
    <p class="card__eyebrow">Client</p>
    <h3><a href="{{ '/client/' | url }}">I want to query one</a></h3>
    <p>
      Somebody already runs a mirror and has given you a read-only connection
      string. Install the client and cross-match against it — no TNS account, no
      SQL, and nothing to operate.
    </p>
  </li>
</ul>

